nd and we would say that it was  granting a legal right where there was  no moral right so Mill will talk about  this in saying that legal rights of  which a person is deprived may be rights  which ought not to have belonged to him  in the first place in the law which  gives that person the  rights may be a bad law allowing  somebody to be a slave of another person  that's a bad law  so mill says in this case we the law is  not the ultimate criterion of justice  but may give to one person a benefit or  impose on another and evil which justice  condemns and then we we talk not in  terms of legal right but he says moral  right so we say a second case of  injustice consists in taking or  withholding from any person that to  which they have a moral right that's a  very important conception of justice the  third he says is very very basic this is  something that when people are put on  the spot and they asked what is justice  and that's a difficult question  they often will respond with some  variation on this saying well it's when  you give people what they deserve and  mill says this doesn't just include  giving people nice things goods it also  includes giving people bad things so if  somebody violates the laws violates  people's rights maybe they deserve to be  punished in that case you're imposing  something bad upon them and that is part  of our conception of justice this can be  done by the way I in in modes that are  not simply retributive but actually look  for some other kind of benefit to accrue  from it the utilitarians were actually  big proponents of punishment that would  look to the future and have some sort of  positive effect for society they weren't  the only ones by the way Thomas Hobbes  talks about that as well in his laws of  nature who they were getting a little  bit far afield but in any case he says  it's universally considered just each  person should obtain that whether good  or evil which he deserves and unjust  that he should obtain a good o